<header><p class="st-kicker">crusader state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Latin Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">1204 CE – 1261 CE · capital Constantinople</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Crusader state founded in Constantinople after the Fourth Crusade sacked the city in 1204, a weak empire that lasted fifty-seven years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, diverted by Venetian interests and Byzantine politics, stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in April 1204 and plundered it. The crusaders elected Baldwin of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-flanders">Flanders</a> emperor and divided the Byzantine lands: one quarter to the emperor, the rest split between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the crusader barons, who received fiefs across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, Macedonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The emperors ruled with Western feudal institutions, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> controlled a share of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and its trade. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> patriarch replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> one, alienating the Greek population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-latin-emperor">Baldwin I</a> was captured by the Bulgarian tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Adrianople</a> in 1205. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-flanders">Henry of Flanders</a> proved an able ruler, but after him the empire shrank to little more than the capital.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-of-constantinople">Baldwin II</a> pawned relics, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-of-thorns">Crown of Thorns</a> sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX</a>, and even his son to raise money. In July 1261 troops of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, Baldwin fled, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> was restored.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/latin-empire.jpg" alt="Latin Empire" width="320" height="169"><figcaption>Image: LatinEmpire · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LatinEmpire2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1204 CE – 1261 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>crusader state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Constantinople</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Baldwin I, Henry of Flanders, Baldwin II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Latin Empire of Constantinople, Empire of Romania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1232&amp;focus=latin-empire" aria-label="Latin Empire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5389 714 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Latin Empire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5709 970L5570 954M5709 970L5709 970M5709 970L5730 989M5709 970L5563 982M5709 970L5530 1071M5709 970L5636 1097M5709 970L5617 1007"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5570 954h0M5709 970h0M5730 989h0M5563 982h0M5530 1071h0M5636 1097h0M5617 1007h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Constantinople. <a href="/atlas?year=1232&amp;focus=latin-empire">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
